crate-jmap-mail-client/src/methods/mod.rs:153-164

    fn ser_opt_keyword_set<S: serde::Serializer>(
        kws: &Option<&[&str]>,
        s: S,
    ) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {
        use serde::ser::SerializeMap;
        let kws = kws.expect("skip_serializing_if guarantees Some");
        ...
    }

The expect() is genuinely unreachable in production because the field carries #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none", serialize_with = "ser_opt_keyword_set")] — serde will not call ser_opt_keyword_set when the field is None.

Stylistic options that some Rust reviewers prefer:
  - if let Some(kws) = kws { ... } else { Err(serde::ser::Error::custom("unreachable")) }
  - match kws.as_ref().ok_or_else(|| serde::ser::Error::custom("..."))? — non-panicking equivalent
  - keep .expect() with an explicit // SAFETY-style comment

Why this is opinion / P4: the precondition is real and the panic is genuinely unreachable; the message names the invariant; and serde custom serializers are widely known to be paired with skip_serializing_if. AGENTS.md says expect() needs a comment explaining why it cannot fail — the message itself names the why ('skip_serializing_if guarantees Some'). A reviewer could go either way on whether that suffices.

Tagged opinion. Filing for completeness; would not block publish.
